\section{Introduction}\label{sec:fixpoint-intro}

The theoretical framework of abstract interpretation guarantees the existence of a least fixedpoint for the abstract semantics, but it does not tell us how to compute it efficiently. This chapter addresses the practical problem of \emph{fixpoint computation}: algorithms for finding the least fixedpoint of a monotone function on a complete lattice. We cover iterative methods, worklist algorithms, chaotic iteration, and strategies for ensuring termination on infinite lattices through partitioning, localization, and narrowing.

\section{Iterative Fixpoint Computation}\label{sec:iterative-fixpoint}

The most basic approach to computing the least fixedpoint of a monotone function $F : L \to L$ is to iterate from the bottom of the lattice:

\begin{definition}[Kleene Iteration]\label{def:kleene}
The \textbf{Kleene iteration} for a monotone function $F$ on a complete lattice $L$ is the sequence:
\[
\bot \sqsubseteq F(\bot) \sqsubseteq F^2(\bot) \sqsubseteq \cdots \sqsubseteq F^n(\bot) \sqsubseteq \cdots
\]
If $L$ satisfies the ascending chain condition (ACC), this sequence reaches a fixedpoint in finitely many steps, and that fixedpoint is the least fixedpoint of $F$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Kleene's Fixed-Point Theorem]\label{thm:kleene}
If $L$ is a complete lattice satisfying ACC and $F : L \to L$ is monotone, then the Kleene iteration converges to $\mathit{lfp}(F)$ in finitely many steps.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By monotonicity, $\bot \sqsubseteq F(\bot)$ implies $F(\bot) \sqsubseteq F^2(\bot)$, and by induction the sequence is ascending. By ACC, the sequence must eventually stabilize at some $F^n(\bot) = F^{n+1}(\bot)$, which is a fixedpoint. By the Knaster--Tarski theorem, this is the least fixedpoint.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Componentwise Iteration}\label{sec:componentwise}

For dataflow analysis, the function $F$ operates on a product lattice $L^{|N|}$ (one component per program point). Componentwise iteration updates one component at a time:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Componentwise Iteration}
\label{alg:componentwise}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Monotone function $F : L^n \to L^n$
\Ensure Least fixedpoint of $F$
\State $x \gets (\bot, \bot, \ldots, \bot)$
\Repeat
  \State $x_{\mathit{old}} \gets x$
  \For{$i = 1$ to $n$}
    \State $x_i \gets F_i(x)$ \Comment{Update component $i$}
  \EndFor
\Until{$x = x_{\mathit{old}}$}
\State \Return $x$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Worklist Algorithms}\label{sec:worklist-fixpoint}

The worklist algorithm (Algorithm~\ref{alg:worklist}) is the standard method for computing dataflow fixedpoints. It maintains a set of ``dirty'' nodes and processes them until no more changes occur.

\subsection{Worklist with Change Detection}\label{sec:worklist-change}

The key optimization is to only process nodes whose inputs have changed:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Worklist with Change Detection}
\label{alg:worklist-change}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Transfer functions $\{f_n\}$, meet operator $\sqcap$
\State Initialize all $\mathit{out}[n] = \bot$
\State $\mathit{worklist} \gets \{n_1, n_2, \ldots, n_k\}$ (all nodes)
\While{$\mathit{worklist} \neq \emptyset$}
  \State $n \gets$ remove from $\mathit{worklist}$
  \State $\mathit{in} \gets \bigsqcap_{m \in \mathit{pred}(n)} \mathit{out}[m]$
  \State $\mathit{new\_out} \gets f_n(\mathit{in})$
  \If{$\mathit{new\_out} \neq \mathit{out}[n]$}
    \State $\mathit{out}[n] \gets \mathit{new\_out}$
    \ForAll{$s \in \mathit{succ}(n)$}
      \If{$s \notin \mathit{worklist}$}
        \State Add $s$ to $\mathit{worklist}$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Worklist Ordering Strategies}\label{sec:worklist-ordering}

The order in which nodes are processed affects the number of iterations:

\begin{definition}[Loop Nesting Forest]\label{def:loop-nesting}
A \textbf{loop nesting forest} represents the hierarchical structure of loops in a CFG. Each loop has a header node, and loops can be nested within other loops. The \emph{loop depth} of a node is its depth in this forest.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Priority by Loop Depth]\label{def:priority-depth}
Process nodes in order of decreasing loop depth: innermost loop nodes first. This ensures that information propagates quickly through the most iterative parts of the program.
\end{definition}

\section{Chaotic Iteration}\label{sec:chaotic-iteration}

\emph{Chaotic iteration} is a generalization of both Kleene iteration and worklist algorithms. It allows arbitrary (but fair) scheduling of component updates.

\begin{definition}[Chaotic Iteration]\label{def:chaotic}
A \textbf{chaotic iteration} for $F : L^n \to L^n$ is a sequence $x^{(0)}, x^{(1)}, x^{(2)}, \ldots$ where each step updates one or more components:
\[
x^{(k+1)}_i = \begin{cases}
  F_i(x^{(k)}) & \text{if component } i \text{ is selected at step } k \\
  x^{(k)}_i & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]
The iteration is \textbf{fair} if every component is selected infinitely often.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Chaotic Iteration Convergence]\label{thm:chaotic-conv}
Any fair chaotic iteration on a lattice satisfying ACC converges to the least fixedpoint of $F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fairness ensures every component is updated infinitely often. Each update either increases the component (in the lattice ordering) or leaves it unchanged. By ACC, each component can increase only finitely many times. Therefore, the iteration must eventually reach a state where no component changes, which is the fixedpoint.
\end{proof}

The worklist algorithm is a special case of chaotic iteration where only components whose inputs have changed are selected.

\section{Partitioning and Localization}\label{sec:partitioning}

For large programs, computing the global fixedpoint is expensive. \emph{Partitioning} divides the program into smaller components that can be analyzed independently or with limited interaction.

\subsection{Weak Partitioning}\label{sec:weak-partitioning}

\begin{definition}[Weak Partitioning]\label{def:weak-partition}
A \textbf{weak partition} of a CFG divides the nodes into blocks $B_1, B_2, \ldots, B_k$ such that information flows between blocks only through a small set of \emph{interface} nodes. Each block can be analyzed independently, with information propagated between blocks through the interfaces.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Strong Partitioning}\label{sec:strong-partitioning}

\begin{definition}[Strong Partition]\label{def:strong-partition}
A \textbf{strong partition} divides the CFG into blocks with \emph{no} information flow between them. Each block can be analyzed completely independently. This is possible when the blocks are in separate strongly connected components with no connecting edges.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Localization}\label{sec:localization}

\begin{definition}[Localization]\label{def:localization}
\textbf{Localization} restricts the analysis to a subset of program points that are relevant to a specific query. For example, to determine whether a variable $x$ is positive at program point $p$, we need only analyze the paths that can reach $p$ and the statements that modify $x$.
\end{definition}

\section{Narrowing Strategies}\label{sec:narrowing-strategies}

\emph{Narrowing} is a technique for recovering precision lost during widening (see Chapter~\ref{sec:widening-narrowing}). After widening has ensured termination, narrowing iteratively refines the solution.

\begin{definition}[Narrowing Operator]\label{def:narrowing-op}
A \textbf{narrowing operator} $\nabla : L \times L \to L$ satisfies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $x \nabla y \sqsubseteq x$ (narrowing decreases or maintains the value)
  \item If $y \sqsubseteq x$, then $y \sqsubseteq x \nabla y$ (narrowing preserves soundness)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Widening with Narrowing}
\label{alg:widen-narrow}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Transfer functions, widening $\Delta$, narrowing $\nabla$
\State Compute fixpoint using widening: $x^w = \mathit{lfp}_\Delta(F)$
\State $x \gets x^w$
\Repeat
  \State $x_{\mathit{old}} \gets x$
  \State $x \gets x \nabla F(x)$ \Comment{Apply narrowing}
\Until{$x = x_{\mathit{old}}$}
\State \Return $x$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Convergence Acceleration}\label{sec:convergence-acceleration}

Several techniques accelerate the convergence of fixpoint computation:

\subsection{Extrapolation}\label{sec:extrapolation}

\begin{definition}[Extrapolation]\label{def:extrapolation}
\textbf{Extrapolation} predicts the limit of an ascending chain from its first few elements, jumping directly to an over-approximation of the limit. This is the essence of widening (Chapter~\ref{sec:widening-narrowing}).
\end{definition}

\subsection{Acceleration for Finite Lattices}\label{sec:finite-acceleration}

For finite lattices, the \emph{transitive closure} of the transfer function can sometimes be computed directly, bypassing iteration:

\begin{definition}[Transfer Function Acceleration]\label{def:tf-accel}
If $f$ is the transfer function for a loop body, the \textbf{accelerated} transfer function $f^*$ computes the effect of iterating the loop any number of times:
\[
f^*(x) = x \sqcup f(x) \sqcup f^2(x) \sqcup \cdots
\]
For some domains (e.g., intervals with simple loop structures), $f^*$ can be computed in closed form.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Acceleration for Intervals]\label{ex:interval-accel}
Consider the loop:
\begin{verbatim}
  i = 0;
  while (i < n) {
    i = i + 1;
  }
\end{verbatim}
The transfer function for the loop body is $f([a, b]) = [a, b + 1]$. The accelerated function is $f^*([0, 0]) = [0, n]$ (assuming $n$ is the upper bound from the loop condition). This computes the final value in one step instead of $n$ iterations.
\end{example}

\subsection{Symbolic Techniques}\label{sec:symbolic-acceleration}

Symbolic techniques use algebraic reasoning to compute fixpoints:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Affine relations}: If all transfer functions are affine, the fixpoint can be computed using linear algebra.
  \item \textbf{Strategy iteration}: For games and fixpoints of monotone functions, strategy iteration algorithms can converge faster than naive iteration.
  \item \textbf{Policy iteration}: Originally from linear programming, adapted for abstract interpretation by Gawalek et al.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Convergence Rate]\label{thm:convergence-rate}
With widening and narrowing, the number of iterations to reach a fixpoint is typically $O(\log h)$ where $h$ is the lattice height, compared to $O(h)$ without acceleration.
\end{theorem}

\begin{historical}
The theory of fixpoint computation in abstract interpretation has evolved from the basic Kleene iteration to sophisticated acceleration techniques. Bourdoncle~\cite{bourdoncle1993efficient} (1993) developed the theory of chaotic iteration and its application to abstract interpretation, including the use of weak topological orderings for optimal iteration strategy. The notion of localization and partitioning was developed by Cousot and Cousot as part of the general theory. Modern work on acceleration includes the use of SMT solvers for computing accelerated transfer functions and the application of policy iteration for numerical abstract domains.
\end{historical}

\section{Weak Topological Orderings}\label{sec:wto}

Bourdoncle~\cite{bourdoncle1993efficient} introduced \emph{weak topological orderings} (WTOs) as an optimal iteration strategy for fixpoint computation.

\begin{definition}[Weak Topological Ordering]\label{def:wto}
A \textbf{weak topological ordering} of a CFG is a hierarchical ordering of nodes that respects the loop structure: loop headers appear before their bodies, and nested loops are properly nested in the ordering. Processing nodes in WTO order ensures that information propagates efficiently through the program.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[WTO Optimality]\label{thm:wto-optimal}
Processing nodes in weak topological order minimizes the number of iterations needed to reach the fixedpoint, compared to any other ordering that respects the loop structure.
\end{theorem}

\section{Distributed and Parallel Fixpoint Computation}\label{sec:parallel-fixpoint}

For large-scale analyses, parallel and distributed computation can significantly reduce analysis time.

\begin{definition}[Parallel Fixpoint Computation]\label{def:parallel-fixpoint}
\textbf{Parallel fixpoint computation} distributes the analysis across multiple processors or machines. Each processor handles a subset of the program's nodes, and synchronization ensures that information propagates correctly across processors.
\end{definition}

Key challenges include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Load balancing}: Distributing work evenly across processors.
  \item \textbf{Communication minimization}: Reducing the amount of data exchanged between processors.
  \item \textbf{Termination detection}: Determining when all processors have reached their local fixedpoints.
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}\label{sec:fixpoint-summary}

Fixpoint computation is the algorithmic heart of abstract interpretation. The basic approach is iterative: start from the bottom of the lattice and repeatedly apply the transfer functions until a fixedpoint is reached. Worklist algorithms optimize this by only processing nodes whose inputs have changed. Chaotic iteration provides a general framework that encompasses all these approaches. For infinite lattices, widening ensures termination, and narrowing recovers precision. Acceleration techniques can dramatically reduce the number of iterations needed. Weak topological orderings provide an optimal iteration strategy. The choice of iteration strategy (ordering, acceleration, partitioning) significantly affects the practical performance of the analysis. For large-scale analyses, parallel and distributed computation can reduce analysis time.

\section{Worklist Algorithms in Detail}\label{sec:worklist-detail}

The worklist algorithm is the standard technique for computing fixpoints efficiently by only processing nodes whose inputs have changed.

\begin{definition}[Worklist Algorithm]\label{def:worklist-alg}
A \textbf{worklist algorithm} maintains a set of ``dirty'' nodes (the worklist) and processes them in some order. When a node is processed, its output is computed and compared with the previous value. If the output changed, the node's successors are added to the worklist.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Generic Worklist Algorithm}
\label{alg:worklist-generic}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG $G = (N, E)$, transfer functions $f_n$ for each $n \in N$, initial values $x_n^0$
\Ensure Fixpoint solution $x^*$
\State $W \gets N$ \Comment{Initialize with all nodes}
\State $x_n \gets x_n^0$ for all $n \in N$
\While{$W \neq \emptyset$}
  \State Select and remove $n$ from $W$ \Comment{Strategy-dependent}
  \State $x_n^{new} \gets f_n(\bigsqcup_{m \in pred(n)} x_m)$
  \If{$x_n^{new} \neq x_n$}
    \State $x_n \gets x_n^{new}$
    \ForAll{$s \in succ(n)$}
      \If{$s \notin W$}
        \State Add $s$ to $W$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Worklist Ordering Strategies}\label{sec:worklist-ordering}

The order in which nodes are selected from the worklist significantly affects the number of iterations:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
\textbf{Strategy} & \textbf{Description} & \textbf{Effect} \\
\hline
FIFO & First-in, first-out & Simple, may do extra work \\
LIFO & Last-in, first-out (stack) & Depth-first, good for nested loops \\
Priority (loop depth) & Process inner loops first & Faster convergence for loop-nests \\
Priority (distance) & Process nodes closest to entry & Good for forward analyses \\
Random & Random selection & Unpredictable performance \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Worklist ordering strategies and their effects.}
\label{tab:worklist-ordering}
\end{table}

\begin{definition}[Loop-Nested Worklist]\label{def:loop-nested-wl}
A \textbf{loop-nested worklist} prioritizes nodes in inner loops over nodes in outer loops. This ensures that inner loops converge before the outer loop processes them, reducing the total number of iterations.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Priority Worklist with Loop Nesting}
\label{alg:priority-worklist}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG with loop nesting forest, transfer functions $f_n$
\Ensure Fixpoint solution
\State Compute loop nesting depths $depth(n)$ for each node
\State $W \gets$ priority queue ordered by $depth(n)$ (deepest first)
\State Add all nodes to $W$
\While{$W \neq \emptyset$}
  \State $n \gets$ extract-max($W$) \Comment{Deepest loop first}
  \State $x_n^{new} \gets f_n(\bigsqcup_{m \in pred(n)} x_m)$
  \If{$x_n^{new} \neq x_n$}
    \State $x_n \gets x_n^{new}$
    \ForAll{$s \in succ(n)$}
      \State insert-or-update($W$, $s$, priority=$depth(s)$)
    \EndFor
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Convergence Analysis}\label{sec:convergence-analysis}

Understanding when and how fixpoint iteration converges is essential for designing efficient analyses.

\subsection{Rate of Convergence}\label{sec:convergence-rate}

\begin{definition}[Convergence Rate]\label{def:convergence-rate}
The \textbf{convergence rate} of an iterative fixpoint computation is the number of iterations needed to reach the fixpoint. For a lattice of height $h$ with $n$ nodes, the worst-case rate is $O(n \cdot h)$ for chaotic iteration.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Round-Robin Convergence]\label{thm:round-robin}
Round-robin iteration (processing all nodes in each round) converges in at most $h$ rounds, where $h$ is the height of the lattice. Each round processes nodes in topological order (if possible) or in an arbitrary order.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Worklist Convergence]\label{thm:worklist-convergence}
A worklist algorithm with loop-nested ordering converges in at most $\sum_{L \in loops} h_L$ iterations, where $h_L$ is the height of the lattice restricted to loop $L$. This is typically much less than $n \cdot h$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Accelerated Convergence}\label{sec:accelerated-convergence}

\begin{definition}[Acceleration by Abstract Fixpoint]\label{def:acceleration}
\textbf{Acceleration} computes the limit of an ascending chain directly, skipping intermediate values. For a sequence $x_0 \sqsubseteq x_1 \sqsubseteq x_2 \sqsubseteq \cdots$, acceleration computes $\bigsqcup_i x_i$ in one step.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Acceleration Example]\label{ex:acceleration}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Without acceleration (interval domain):
  // Iteration 1: x ∈ [0, 1]
  // Iteration 2: x ∈ [0, 2]
  // Iteration 3: x ∈ [0, 3]
  // ... (many iterations until widening kicks in)
  
  // With acceleration:
  // Detect pattern: upper bound increases by 1 each iteration
  // Accelerate: x ∈ [0, +∞]  (jump directly to the limit)
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\begin{definition}[Narrowing as Convergence Acceleration]\label{def:narrowing-accel}
\textbf{Narrowing} accelerates convergence from above: after widening produces an over-approximation, narrowing iteratively refines it by intersecting with the result of one more application of the transfer function.
\end{definition}

\section{Partitioned Fixpoint Computation}\label{sec:partitioned-fixpoint}

For large programs, the fixpoint computation can be partitioned into independent subproblems.

\begin{definition}[Independent Subproblems]\label{def:independent-sub}
Two sets of program points $S_1, S_2$ are \textbf{independent} if no dataflow information flows between them. The fixpoint computation can be solved separately for $S_1$ and $S_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Slicing for Partitioning]\label{def:slicing-partition}
\textbf{Program slicing} identifies independent subsets of the analysis. A backward slice from a variable $v$ at point $p$ includes all program points that may affect $v$ at $p$. Non-overlapping slices can be analyzed independently.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Partitioned Fixpoint Computation}
\label{alg:partitioned-fixpoint}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG $G$, variable set $V$, analysis $\mathcal{A}$
\Ensure Fixpoint solution for each partition
\State Compute dependence graph $D$ for $\mathcal{A}$
\State Find connected components $C_1, C_2, \ldots, C_k$ of $D$
\For{$i = 1$ to $k$}
  \State Solve fixpoint for nodes in $C_i$ independently
\EndFor
\State Merge results from all partitions
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{theorem}[Partitioning Speedup]\label{thm:partitioning-speedup}
If the fixpoint computation can be partitioned into $k$ independent subproblems of sizes $n_1, \ldots, n_k$, the total work is $\sum_{i=1}^{k} O(n_i \cdot h)$, compared to $O((\sum n_i) \cdot h)$ for the monolithic computation. When the $n_i$ are balanced, this gives a $k$-fold speedup.
\end{theorem}

\section{Fixpoint Computation with Change Detection}\label{sec:change-detection}

Efficient fixpoint computation requires detecting when values have actually changed, to avoid unnecessary re-processing.

\begin{definition}[Sparse Evaluation]\label{def:sparse-eval}
\textbf{Sparse evaluation} represents dataflow values only at points where they are defined or used, rather than at every program point. This reduces the number of values that need to be tracked and compared.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Incremental Change Detection]\label{def:incremental-change}
\textbf{Incremental change detection} maintains a \emph{delta} (difference) between the current and previous values. Only the delta is propagated, reducing the cost of join operations.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Incremental Fixpoint with Delta Propagation}
\label{alg:incremental-fixpoint}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG, transfer functions, initial values
\Ensure Fixpoint solution
\State $x_n \gets x_n^0$ for all $n$; $\delta_n \gets x_n^0$ for all $n$
\State $W \gets \{n \mid \delta_n \neq \bot\}$
\While{$W \neq \emptyset$}
  \State Remove $n$ from $W$
  \State $\Delta_{in} \gets \bigsqcup_{m \in pred(n)} \delta_m$
  \State $x_n^{new} \gets f_n(x_n \sqcup \Delta_{in})$
  \State $\delta_n^{new} \gets x_n^{new} \setminus x_n$ \Comment{New information only}
  \If{$\delta_n^{new} \neq \bot$}
    \State $x_n \gets x_n^{new}$; $\delta_n \gets \delta_n^{new}$
    \ForAll{$s \in succ(n)$}
      \State Add $s$ to $W$
    \EndFor
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Fixpoint Computation for Specific Analyses}\label{sec:fixpoint-specific}

\subsection{Constant Propagation Fixpoint}\label{sec:constprop-fixpoint}

For constant propagation, the lattice is the flat lattice of constants: $\bot \sqsubset c \sqsubset \top$ for each constant $c$. The height is 2, so fixpoint iteration converges in at most 2 rounds per variable.

\begin{definition}[Constant Propagation Transfer]\label{def:constprop-transfer}
The transfer function for an assignment $v = e$ in constant propagation:
\[
val^{new}(v) = \begin{cases}
\bot & \text{if any operand is } \bot \\
c & \text{if all operands are constants and } e \text{ evaluates to } c \\
\top & \text{otherwise (non-constant operands)}
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Reaching Definitions Fixpoint}\label{sec:reaching-fixpoint}

For reaching definitions, the lattice is $\wp(D)$ (powerset of definitions) with union as join. The height is $|D|$, and each iteration can add at least one definition, so convergence takes at most $|D|$ rounds.

\begin{definition}[Reaching Definitions Transfer]\label{def:reaching-transfer}
For a basic block $B$ with definitions $def(B)$ and kills $kill(B)$:
\[
out(B) = gen(B) \cup (in(B) \setminus kill(B))
\]
where $gen(B)$ is the set of definitions generated in $B$ and $kill(B)$ is the set of definitions killed by $B$.
\end{definition}

\section{Termination Guarantees}\label{sec:fixpoint-termination}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Lattice Termination]\label{thm:finite-lattice-term}
On a finite-height lattice of height $h$ with $n$ nodes, chaotic iteration with monotone transfer functions terminates in at most $n \cdot h$ steps.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Worklist Termination]\label{thm:worklist-term}
A worklist algorithm terminates if: (1) the lattice has finite height, (2) transfer functions are monotone, and (3) every node that changes is eventually re-processed.
\end{theorem}
